      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "DNS changes take time to propagate because of a process called DNS caching, which is a massive, decentralized system of servers around the world. Even after you've updated your A records, it can take anywhere from a few minutes to 48 hours for every single recursive resolver on the internet to update their records. Think of it like a massive, slow-moving stampede of information across the entire globe. You'll need to wait for the Time To Live (TTL) settings on your old records to expire everywhere.",
