    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically just waiting on the TTL to hit zero? is there any way to force it to update faster or is it just out of my hands once the records are set?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right, it's largely a matter of waiting for the TTL to expire, which is the instruction telling other servers how long they can safely cache your old information. While you can't *force* the global network to update instantly, you can try to minimize the wait time by setting a very low TTL (like 300 seconds or less) *before* you make the actual change. This tells the world's servers, \"Hey, check back on this address very soon!\" But even with a low TTL, some stubborn caches will still take a little while to clear out.",
      "source": "target",
